    "publication": "Cancer cell 2019",
    "description": "SUMMARY We performed genomic and transcriptomic sequencing of 133 combined hepatocellular and intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma (cHCC-ICC) cases, including separate, combined, and mixed subtypes.",
    "meta_description": "SUMMARY We performed genomic and transcriptomic sequencing of 133 combined hepatocellular and intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma (cHCC-ICC) cases, including separate, combined, and mixed subtypes.",
    "primary_site": "Liver",
    "cohort_description": "Pan-Asia multi-center cohort of 133 sequenced cHCC-ICC patients, including separate, combined, and mixed histologic subtypes, with matched normal controls and multi-omic profiling.",
    "data_repositories": [],
    "corresponding_authors": "* Correspondence: [redacted-email] (H.N.), [redacted-email] (M.-S.Z.), [redacted-email] (F.B.), [redacted-email]",
    "key_findings": [
      "Combined and mixed cHCC-ICC are distinct molecular subtypes, with combined tumors more ICC-like and mixed tumors more HCC-like.",
      "TP53, AXIN1, RB1, PTEN, ARID2, and BRD7 were significantly mutated, while CTNNB1 and KRAS mutations were notably scarce compared with HCC and ICC.",
      "Separate-type cHCC-ICC showed both monoclonal and multiclonal origins, whereas combined and mixed types showed predominantly monoclonal origin.",
      "Single-nucleus sequencing of a mixed-type tumor confirmed monoclonal origin with subclonal diversification.",
      "Nestin expression was significantly elevated in cHCC-ICC and may serve as a diagnostic and prognostic biomarker."
    ]
  },
